Alli "Maybe you should ask _pointers_ if they know how _I_ work." McDermitt
Program 2: Tar Archiver
CS3411 Spring 2025

The program works on everything I've tested it for. I'm pretty sure I hit every major edge case.
I've tested for:
 - 3 file ctar (one header file, minus one) and subsequent utar
 - 4 file ctar (one header file exactly) and subsequent utar
 - 5 file ctar (two header files) and subsequent utar
 - 50 file ctar and subsequent utar
 - 3 file ctar and 1 append (append into first header) and subsequent utar
 - 4 file ctar and 1 append (append into new header) and subsequent utar
 - 5 file ctar and 1 append (append into second existing header) and subsequent utar
 - 50 file ctar, 50 file append, and subsequent utar
 - empty ctar, 6 file append, and subsequent utar
 - delete file from first header and subsequent utar
 - delete file from second header and subsequent utar
 - delete everything from two headers and subsequent utar

The commands with which I tested all of these are available in my shell testing suite, `test.sh`,
which I've graciously included. It's admittedly bit overkill for this purpose, which I only
mention because the syntax over there can be confusing, but it is all there, and it would be 
needlessly time-consuming to rewrite them all here if they're all there too. It also leaves the
test folders in place, so if it's really that confusing, just run the script, and you can see for
yourself the whole series of outputs.

I also tested single file ctar+utar and empty ctar+utar, but didn't make test cases for them.
We live and we learn.

I've also tested pretty much all of the possible error messages, but I think a few of them may 
have become vestigial as I cleared up edge cases in the end phases of development. And finally,
I've valgrind-ed (valground?) pretty much every case I could think of.

♥

Addendum for Github: This is a file archiver, which uses two files: "ctar" creates ctar files, and
"utar" retrieves the files from within. "ctar" can also append and remove files to preexisting
.ctar files. There is no actual compression of the files, but the archiving capabilites are
completely functional, as far as the 12 test cases are concerned.

This folder also includes a Shell script, "test.sh", to test basic functionality.
